(a) The city shall expend, in the fiscal year during which the tax is first collected at a rate in excess of three percent and at all times thereafter, an amount equal to the amount by which the total taxes collected under this article exceed the taxes which would be collected at a rate of three percent pursuant to O.C.G.A. § 48-13-51(a)(3) for:
(1) Promoting tourism, conventions or trade shows;
(2) Supporting a facility owned or operated by a state authority for convention and trade show purposes or any other similar or related purposes;
(3) Supporting a facility owned or operated by a local government or authority for convention and trade show purposes or any other similar or related purposes, if a written agreement to provide such support was in effect on January 1, 1987, and if such facility is substantially completed and in operation prior to July 1, 1987;
(4) Supporting a facility owned or operated by a local government or local authority for convention and trade show purposes or any other similar or related purposes if construction of such facility is funded or was funded on or after July 1, 1990, in whole or in part by a grant of state funds or is funded on or after July 1, 1990, in whole or substantially by an appropriation of state funds;
(5) Supporting a facility owned by a local government or authority for convention and trade show purposes if construction of such facility is substantially funded or was substantially funded on or after February 28, 1985, by a special county one percent sales and use tax authorized by Article 3 of Chapter 8 of this title;
(6) For some combination of such purposes.
Amounts so expended shall be expended only through a contract or contracts with the state, a department of state government, a state authority, a convention and visitors bureau authority created by local act of the General Assembly for a municipality, or a private sector nonprofit organization, or through a contract with some combination of such entities, except that amounts expended for purposes of subsections (a)(3) and (4) of this section may be so expended in any otherwise lawful manner.
(b) For purposes of this article, a "private sector nonprofit organization" shall be a chamber of commerce, a convention and visitors' bureau, a regional travel association or any other private group organized for similar purposes which is exempt from federal income tax under section 501(c) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1987.
(Ord. of 6-21-2005, § 1)

1045:
Every person, firm, company or corporation engaged in business as of January 1 of the taxable year, and subject to the occupational tax shall, on or before February 15 of each year, make a return and application for an occupational tax certificate in writing to the licensing and revenue officer or his designee, in the form and manner required as follows:
(1) Name of the person, firm, company or corporation, including the complete address of the resident agent or other responsible official of the business;
(2) Location, telephone and mailing address at which the trade, business or profession is to be conducted;
(3) Exact nature of the trade, business or profession for which the occupational tax certificate is requested;
(4) Other information as shall be required by the city in order to compute the occupational tax, including, without limitation, the number of registered or licensed professionals, previous calendar or fiscal year gross receipts, projections of gross receipts for the current taxable year, federal tax identification or social security account number, or other information as may be required for the administration of this article;
(5) All such information required to be given by the owner, a member of the firm, an officer of the corporation or an authorized employee, agent or accountant, having knowledge of actual business done, which application shall identify the individual making the return and shall be certified to be true, correct and complete to the best of his knowledge and belief.
(Ord. No. 810-A, art. A, § 8, 11-29-1994)
